#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486} :activity/id #uuid "90bcccea-5782-59d1-a524-d6852abe26cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780 :topic/id #uuid "be10c23c-41f3-53b5-9245-d50fb098c325" :topic/math-academy-id 1106} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097837 :tutorial/content "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg)), and another point ![](images/42/42193b6382254991fb9c053ead9edd0631767155ea51d8a8551cfc4fc734340e.svg) the vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) is called the **position vector** of the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg)\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/41/412bd151cdf5602575028591e2f87d9a7e0328aa6b18dccb6459e7041a5d9b9c.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) by first moving from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and then from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/ac/ac96d55fd0d74cf5fd24f1f1a96beb3865bc70bc45d17be5aad209c5adb1be6d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/e2/e283cb6e7ab325c89dd652abd93ff1382b714462887d8839dfed8769693cc7a7.svg) we get\n![](images/e1/e1bde038ef63f77c3ecc5357b49999c96dd6d331aa7455fcdd1935b1f3eb8261.svg)\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." :tutorial/id #uuid "055562c0-b02b-58fe-93a0-5e216241c04f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e10b4911-140a-5838-a16e-13feb12fba7b" :step/math-academy-id 661397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420467}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} :step/id #uuid "e9410dda-1b4e-5dca-b7ed-64fdcc297e4e" :step/math-academy-id 661408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420465}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "eb2bab86-218f-5b3a-9a4e-b0b3872ba6db" :step/math-academy-id 661411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} :step/id #uuid "74a3f281-5081-51cb-a589-0b5531ea612f" :step/math-academy-id 661399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/92/92e98c3630891023a50149ee1f15d2bd5d2730e93f166931e86a9e4640ebd1c1.svg) and a parallelogram ![](images/14/14fa5b1a9ad8e22445d2e6ab97a047b0b6dc51912ed129823847560bad78554f.svg) where ![](images/37/37252d39c3cb979bda5aa0e19184009625e32ad6b4d496ec843edcd9ebd11444.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/1e/1ea9147123ec18b9ae6f584584c919a462f7efdee6b5b72b61a1cf3c4b13b88a.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) From the diagram, we can write\n![](images/8c/8c0197f45331c0bfd19524c6044f35ddd1d725512112d9700750d10c8709f89f.svg)\n\nWe know that\n![](images/14/146676b86f179710731d5e1ec1b4e5e5e3cf4cdf1af0d4cd4db3d709a36ea0c6.svg)\n\nAlso, since ![](images/c6/c6dbfaa7b99647ca21ec1ad2c57f563756ed17cec04b559d89a086571f1419a1.svg) is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n![](images/94/940eac51696aa93c499bd105cf5726bbed6eaac94eda297d0743af6cd00a67d3.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/a6/a69eca8aae37a4ecd6d3653c994b2bea69e37a8be6dcb00c4a5eeafbc58f1382.svg) we get\n![](images/f5/f56355c56683deba996e3f8cbdd8a9862cd7a9f6f570be9ab12cd4520748e7ec.svg)\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "bd7a9db3-e422-5170-83a1-857ca34b9bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15988 :tutorial/title "Writing a Position Vector Using Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "57d87818-8242-532e-8177-977c7fa7906e" :step/math-academy-id 661398 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362403 :tutorial/content "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that ![](images/80/80eb0b356da6e6a2315ea1466d34b914c6cad5e59ff07c4d061ea82b69ed9838.svg) and we are asked to find ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo find ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we want to match the expression\n![](images/52/52599a11cea864ea801b87d8ec0ea288252afa4265395d9fbb4a4dc2815e9e7e.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) be the origin, and let ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/a1/a14e933631f2b075c5a0bb4ab14fad2ca2790c3be2122b1fe8f4b7193fb505b2.svg) end at points ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/21/2109f0ae1a3679002930f1bf8bfa3490cf23c540ae6bbd50c093e5064327ab15.svg) as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is ![](images/bd/bdd7762354dbbdecb02ccf8284e0830f5421588bbb7fbf1bd9c3a9268a750649.svg) we first draw the vector ![](images/cb/cbbad9d9d0e183ac112cfdf946b99fd00718e0c6856d1a75564925b0e2760e85.svg) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/a2/a2b2f82b89941e4065ecf4c3bf612ab3d7e66fdb16c848dd2ce169480714e7ae.svg) Then\n\n![](images/3a/3a9a2959c3a256cb2651e0f00b0d7fe774f95aeee92289284bffb4c3b11b843a.svg)\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n![](images/f1/f1e5980924989b13362a77d776ecbd4b7567f99e30fe26eb57ee99d18fa1d464.svg)\n\nAlso, the vector ![](images/5f/5f1f2cecb3a462fc45e60ec717c2fde5b1aaba79448a58aae1a9d3023b21b7bb.svg) has length ![](images/f5/f551620762fb84e33026129595b42ef514082cc2e137b6762d75fd574d56383c.svg) but points in the opposite direction to ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/4f/4fdd6b0f88ff6e5ea11ace72a5b61c24fe8cf6b1d85b4bceee599dde18471b5d.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/3c/3cce8321317b15087f61b778b47963fdff160e7df1e3ade0d33b040e10125994.svg) we get\n\n![](images/71/71d018c5f48d3afb47781ddff61419623e8c6d15856151398905494498254880.svg)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/57897701facaf26cf0826a2b6a12f408ae1acd8a5f4e78575a4457f66a156d9b.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e6/e6090f180976846ce7a2e6e8d696a0fa078a471c950de6188908528c881839fb.svg) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/79/792ff38b4658d1d4bbfbc57cbe93be95dd8d90b24559f601250eaa81becf8d24.svg) we get\n\n![](images/99/99914dd947bc5c5acbb4d9aa989efb9fef4dc78b0242629ea0508a81e7058f61.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5155eff-3506-551b-8dc0-597bff07650f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15990 :tutorial/title "Vectors in Opposite Directions"} :step/id #uuid "d0bece94-821b-52ea-b6b1-d2373c5447e0" :step/math-academy-id 661404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235582}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"} :step/id #uuid "d4d86c29-1b09-51e8-ae95-b9a5b6ceb242" :step/math-academy-id 661405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487}}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096886}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420468} :activity/id #uuid "bf7c0114-33c8-5bb5-b8a8-2b8fac8eb7ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780 :topic/id #uuid "be10c23c-41f3-53b5-9245-d50fb098c325" :topic/math-academy-id 1106} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1fbbe3-94d8-5bd3-8727-4e6f9a832097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420448} {:answer/id #uuid "90599886-7bfb-5b72-84ac-b60df87c5d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420452} {:answer/id #uuid "a6826047-3513-5e97-9ed8-1ae795633fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420453} {:answer/id #uuid "b05969db-3a13-5b96-96be-8b739e33d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420455} {:answer/id #uuid "cf620219-7b28-5e23-a450-b4abd046ba47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420453} :answer-field/id #uuid "979e9c27-4560-5e47-8337-19ce5dc8192c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5f98ecb-0f26-50ae-9a82-cc334fd82c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-20413" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bb7598c9ebae28be572508974cd025caa8f4a6c3313f025ff0f5ca55296334e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/43642acdb991827b4e1e16744e8a69507a383a1319ae07efde78480af4bab166.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ and $g$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=2g.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=3|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-3f.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =2g-3f \\\\  & =-3f+2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-3f+2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$"} :step/id #uuid "509e4633-305b-52c6-89d7-db63ec41fd87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} {:answer/id #uuid "633d97e3-8d84-57c9-8ac7-bdd2378d9d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420449} {:answer/id #uuid "c2051bdd-22cb-509c-8fb6-340e90eb858d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4325a-2b33-5c98-a020-248e21f996f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420459} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d19e05-07c5-5adc-9a1c-131ee586eacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f94aaa-a8cf-5aa4-9f93-884e35c10a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b667d36f-710b-5326-b636-9597ec1ec3f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77230" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3a.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=2b+\\lambda a=2b+3a.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "561c8240-56f6-5b3d-9001-524cd68b5a86" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420470}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "747da426-8469-5cfb-b9c1-0f275aa12f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420450} {:answer/id #uuid "7870ed39-d3a3-5476-9037-af3a46374a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{5}{8}a+\\frac{3}{8}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420451} {:answer/id #uuid "ac24f6e2-146b-56c4-926e-7b7b203e75f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{8}a+\\frac{5}{8}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420454} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a4f00d-a543-5fec-976f-fd2b59d619f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{4}a+\\frac{1}{4}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420458} {:answer/id #uuid "f78f9d21-45cd-5cd7-95b8-055e70bef457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{7}{8}a+\\frac{1}{8}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420451} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd7bc1d-4953-5e13-8ab6-2c158bebc612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fe46ce7-a162-566e-a261-116569794a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-120289" :question/problem "![](images/52/52e25ccc5b59b3eecd914c5b141e443475a5de58299404528d27f1fc85db97ea.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$, and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=3:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=3:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{3}{8}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{3}{8}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{8}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}a+\\frac{3}{8}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6fb35957-a4df-5eb9-90fe-225f93ca7c52"}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420446}]}
